If you are absolutely certain the ST server has pulled the latest code and successfully restarted, but it is *still* throwing MismatchedInputException or UnrecognizedPropertyException, then we are dealing with a notorious Spring Boot configuration trap.
I looked back at your very first code snippet (Full code (1).txt), and I found the **"Silent Killer"**.
### The Root Cause: The AppConfig.java Trap
In your AppConfig.java, you defined a custom Jackson ObjectMapper like this:
```java
@Bean
@Primary
public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
    ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
    mapper.registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());
    mapper.disable(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS);
    return mapper;
}

```
**Here is why this is crashing your ST server:** By using new ObjectMapper(), you completely wiped out Spring Boot's default safety nets. Spring Boot natively configures Jackson to ignore unknown properties and accept empty strings. Your custom bean overwrote that with Jackson's default strict rules. In a deployed environment where proxies, gateways, or React might sneak in an empty string ("") or a hidden header field, this strict ObjectMapper panics and throws an IOException instantly.
### The Ultimate Fix (Apply these 2 changes)
#### 1. Fix AppConfig.java
You must manually re-enable the safety nets in your custom ObjectMapper bean. Update your AppConfig.java to this exactly:
```java
package com.fincore.VoucherService.config;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.DeserializationFeature;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.SerializationFeature;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.datatype.jsr310.JavaTimeModule;
import lombok.extern.slf4j.Slf4j;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Primary;

@Configuration
@Slf4j
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    @Primary
    public ObjectMapper objectMapper() {
        log.info("APP_CONFIG_INIT | Configuring Primary Jackson ObjectMapper");

        ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
        mapper.registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());
        mapper.disable(SerializationFeature.WRITE_DATES_AS_TIMESTAMPS);

        // --- NEW: THE ULTIMATE SAFETY NETS ---
        // 1. Forces Jackson to ignore ANY extra/garbage fields sent by ST or React
        mapper.configure(DeserializationFeature.FAIL_ON_UNKNOWN_PROPERTIES, false);
        
        // 2. Forces Jackson to treat empty strings ("") as null instead of crashing
        mapper.configure(DeserializationFeature.ACCEPT_EMPTY_STRING_AS_NULL_OBJECT, true);
        
        // 3. Allows single values to be read as arrays (prevents mismatch crashes)
        mapper.configure(DeserializationFeature.ACCEPT_SINGLE_VALUE_AS_ARRAY, true);

        return mapper;
    }
}

```
#### 2. Fix BatchPostingDto.java (Inner Class Trap)
Lombok's @Data annotation sometimes fails to generate a default constructor for static inner classes, which Jackson *requires* to deserialize lists of objects (like your rows array).
Open your BatchPostingDto.java and explicitly add @NoArgsConstructor and @AllArgsConstructor to the inner JournalRequestRow class:
```java
    // ... outer class fields ...

    @Data
    @NoArgsConstructor      // <--- ADD THIS
    @AllArgsConstructor     // <--- ADD THIS
    @JsonIgnoreProperties(ignoreUnknown = true)
    public static class JournalRequestRow {
        @JsonProperty("branch")
        private String branch;
        
        // ... rest of the fields ...
    }

```
### Why this will finally end the nightmare:
By forcing these settings directly into the ObjectMapper bean, **Jackson physically cannot throw an UnrecognizedPropertyException anymore.** It is globally disabled across your entire application.
Push these two tiny configuration changes to ST. This will force Jackson to become completely bulletproof against whatever the React UI or the ST API Gateway is throwing at it.
